    <other_details id="References">Archetypes: openEHR-DEMOGRAPHIC-PARTY_RELATIONSHIP.party_membership.v1, openEHR-DEMOGRAPHIC-PARTY_RELATIONSHIP.role_assignment.v1, openEHR-DEMOGRAPHIC-PERSON.generic_person.v1, openEHR-EHR-CLUSTER.address.v1, openEHR-EHR-CLUSTER.electronic_communication.v1, openEHR-EHR-CLUSTER.structured_name.v1. Structure derived from the openEHR CKM demographic drafts (openEHR-DEMOGRAPHIC-PERSON.person.v0, PERSON.person-patient.v0, PARTY_IDENTITY.person_name.v0, ROLE.individual_provider.v0, CAPABILITY.individual_credentials.v0). Names, addresses, telecom and contact persons use the published CKM clusters openEHR-EHR-CLUSTER.structured_name.v1, address.v1, electronic_communication.v1 and person.v1. Aligned with the HL7 FHIR R4 (4.0.1) resources Patient, Practitioner and RelatedPerson (https://hl7.org/fhir/R4/).</other_details>
    <details>
      <language>
        <terminology_id>
          <value>ISO_639-1</value>
        </terminology_id>
        <code_string>en</code_string>
      </language>
      <purpose>A care provider as a party: the person's names, identifiers such as professional registration numbers, administrative gender, qualifications, work contacts and languages, with memberships of care teams and organisations and the roles the person holds.</purpose>
      <keywords>care provider</keywords>
      <keywords>practitioner</keywords>
      <keywords>healthcare professional</keywords>
      <keywords>staff</keywords>
      <keywords>qualification</keywords>
      <use>Use for a member of staff who provides care, such as a physician, a nurse or a therapist. A membership puts the person in a care team or an organisation; a role assignment gives the person a shared function role. Patients reach the person through their own care relationships.</use>
      <misuse>Not for the patient side of a care provider who is also a patient: that is a separate PERSON-Patient.v1 party.</misuse>
      <copyright>© openehr-reference-content contributors</copyright>
    </details>
  </description>
